\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 ABSTRACT}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 In the age of weaponized disinformation, the question for democracies is not merely who will win an election, but whether the outcome will be accepted as legitimate. To assess the challenge faced by U.S. electoral officials in convincing the public of the security of election procedure we conducted a survey experiment on a national sample of 4987 U.S. adults in the lead-up to the 2020 election. Subjects were exposed to claims about voter fraud as well as crisis communication counter-messaging attributed to election officials. We find that regardless of the messaging strategy, subjects were unmoved by the counter-messaging with partisanship being a clear predictor of increased skepticism towards election security. Our findings illustrate the difficulties election officials face in convincing the publics about election legitimacy and highlight the systemic dangers posed by electoral disinformation.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb240\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\ql\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 FULL TEXT}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb240\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\ql\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Introduction}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Democracies survive not because elections are held, but because candidates, parties, and populations accept their results (Anderson et al., 2005). The spread of false or misleading information about election procedures can enflame concerns about the fairness of elections and undermine popular belief in the legitimacy of an election\u8217 s outcome (e.g. Berlinski et al., 2021; Clayton et al., 2021; Zimmermann & Kohring, 2020). But the corrosive impact of stories about electoral malpractice may have wider implications for citizens\u8217  faith in democracy itself (Norris, 2019). Anticipating this problem, during the 2020 Presidential election, U.S. election officials adopted a strategy of publicly refuting electoral disinformation.1 This marked a key evolution in the role of election officials like Secretaries of State (SoS) from simple election administrators to public defenders of the democratic process.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 But did these interventions actually matter? Against this real-world exercise in electoral legitimacy, we assess the effects of messaging from election officials in boosting public confidence in election procedures. This research builds on the study of crisis communications and attempts to leverage best practices in political arenas like election security (Berger et al., 2018). In particular, we gauge how effective strategies like message-transparency (Holland et al., 2018) are in mitigating electoral apprehensions fueled by disinformation within the context of partisan polarization and competing cues about electoral integrity. Unfortunately, we find that messaging from election officials has no significant effects at assuaging the electoral anxieties stoked by disinformation regardless of transparency level.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb240\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\ql\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Countering Disinformation}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24
\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Countering disinformation is inherently difficult in the digital age (e.g. Brashier et al., 2021). Not only is there no magic messaging bullet to mitigate disinformation, but corrective efforts may actually produce unintended and harmful consequences. Preemptive countermeasures, such as attaching a \u8220 questionable\u8221  or \u8220 false\u8221  warning to disinformation, may actually lessen trust in the warning itself (e.g. Clayton et al., 2020; Freeze et al., 2021; Pennycook et al., 2020). And, although backfire effects do not always occur, the influence of false information may linger long after corrective messages go public (Nyhan, 2021; Thorson, 2016).}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Despite these difficulties, electoral disinformation can neither be ignored nor allowed to fester unimpeded. Since the 2016 U.S. presidential election, the issue of electoral disinformation and its deleterious effects has garnered significant scholarly attention (e.g. Albertson & Guiler, 2020; Bovet & Makse, 2019). Whereas studies have examined the role of the media in refuting spurious claims of election fraud (e.g. Berlinski et al., 2021; Bailard et al., 2022), the role played by election officials themselves has been largely overlooked. Officials like Secretaries of State have witnessed an evolution in their responsibilities from administrators of the vote to crisis communicators who are tasked with providing the public with accurate information about electoral processes and procedures amid a highly fractured information environment (Benson, 2016; Hale & Brown, 2020; Montjoy, 2010). This transition became prominent in 2020, when changes in voting procedures stemming from the Covid-19 and the promoting of electoral conspiracy theories by partisan elites (Benkler et al., 2020; Justwan & Williamson, 2022) required officials to adopt more public roles as defenders of the electoral process (e.g. Meet the Press 2020).}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Although this public relations role is new for election officials, effective crisis messaging in other situations has been effective at assuaging public concerns and a key mechanism for shaping stakeholder perceptions (e.g. Bundy et al., 2017; Coombs, 2015; James, 2011). Throughout the 2020 election, efforts were made to leverage these practices in crisis communications to address uncertainty about voting procedures and bolster electoral legitimacy (i.e. Berger et al., 2018). Of particular importance, is the concept of transparency (Schnackenberg & Tomlinson, 2016) and how it can be applied both by an organization at-large (Albu & Flyverborn 2019; Christensen & Cheney, 2015) as well as in individual messaging (Holland et al., 2018; Holland et al., 2021) to buoy public confidence. Specifically, the perceived sharing of high-quality information is associated with positive attributes including trust, credibility, and accountability (Craft & Heim, 2008; Craig et al., 2016; Rawlins, 2008; Sisson, 2017; Tsetsura & Aziz, 2018). Such reactions are not only the qualities that election officials hoped to engender towards voting procedures but are potentially key attributes for rebuffing electoral disinformation. The effective \u8220 debunking\u8221  of disinformation has been closely tied to the ability of fact-checkers to gain the trust of the audience (Brandtzaeg & F\'f8lstad, 2017). Through transparent messaging that emphasizes clarity, disclosure, and accuracy (Schnackenberg & Tomlinson, 2016), election officials can obtain audience trust and cultivate the credibility needed to refute disinformation (Humprecht, 2020).}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Yet, whereas the existing literature on transparency in crisis communications has examined the effects of this strategy in different types of crises (e.g. Claeys & Cauberghe, 2014; Coombs & Holladay, 2002; Holland et al., 2021), electoral crisis communication occur in an explicitly political environment that present unique messaging challenges. Notably, messages from election officials must contend with polarization in the information environment and rival political cues (Jerit & Barabas, 2012). Tellingly, despite instances of electoral fraud being rare in the United States, popular belief in electoral malfeasance continues to persist (Ahlquist et al., 2014). Moreover, underlying conspiratorial thinking can stoke beliefs in electoral fraud which can be exacerbated by partisan reasoning and elite-cue signaling (Edelson et al., 2017; Enders et al., 2021). Crisis communication literature anticipates complications from competing information sources, especially social media (Jin et al., 2014), but it has considered neither partisanship nor competing elite-cues as countervailing forces. While citizens can eschew partisanship in favor of substantive information in certain circumstances (Bullock, 2011; Boudreau & MacKenzie, 2014), the evidence of partisan effects on political perceptions remains strong (e.g. Cohen, 2003; Petersen et al., 2013) and can color how individuals digest information. For example, as Druckman et al. (2013) show, elite partisan polarization decreases citizen use of substantive information in favor of conformance with elite perspectives. In the context of elections and their perceived legitimacy, this suggests that no matter what election officials say or how transparent their messaging is, politically congruent cues may be the dominant influence on public perceptions.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb240\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\ql\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Theoretical Expectations}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24
\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 The purpose of our study is to test the effectiveness of electoral crisis communications in buttressing perceptions of election procedures amid a disinformation campaign and gauge whether specific strategies like message transparency can be effective tools for sustaining confidence in electoral legitimacy. In line with existing literature on the effects of disinformation, we expect any exposure to electoral disinformation will have a deleterious effect on perceptions of election procedures (e.g. McKay & Tenove, 2021).}
{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 H}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0
\ulnone\cf2 1}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 : All subjects in treatment groups containing disinformation will express less confidence in the election\u8217 s than subjects in our control group.}
{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 However, the general crisis communication literature leads us to expect disinformation effects to be blunted by any crisis communication interventions made by election officials (Claeys & Cauberghe, 2014).}
{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 H}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0
\ulnone\cf2 2}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 : Subjects in treatment groups with SoS messages (TG2 & TG3) will express greater confidence in election security than subjects exposed to disinformation but no responses from officials (TG1).}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Furthermore, the literature\u8217 s emphasis on transparency suggests that the \u8220 high transparency\u8221  messaging (Holland et al., 2021) that outlines specific actions being taken to redress concerns, should be the most effective at boosting confidence in electoral legitimacy. As such we would expect to see a difference in subject perceptions based on the degree of transparency included in the SoS counter-messaging.}
{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 H}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0
\ulnone\cf2 3}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 : Subjects in the High Transparency treatment group (TG3) will express greater confidence in electoral legitimacy than subjects in the Low Transparency group (TG2).}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Lastly, in line with our expectation about partisanship\u8217 s impact on perceptions of electoral security, we anticipate heterogeneous partisan effects to be evident in the experiment. Specifically, given elite cues prior to the 2020 election, we anticipate Republicans to be both more receptive to stories questioning election security that aligned with partisan narratives (Druckman et al., 2013) and less sympathetic to counter-messaging from election officials. This will be different from Democrats who will be more responsive towards news sources and messaging from election officials since it counters the theories being championed by Donald Trump.2}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 H}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 4}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 : Republicans will be less likely to have confidence in election procedures than Democrats regardless of treatment groups.}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb240\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum
\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\ql\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Research Design}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}
\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0
\ulnone\cf2 As part of a national survey experiment fielded prior to the 2020 general election, we assessed crisis messaging effects from election officials. Given the natural political context in which the experiment occurred, we use the \u8220 real world contamination\u8221  of pre-treatment effects that Gaines et al. (2007) raised as part of our design assumptions. Specifically, our randomly assigned crisis communication treatments were preceded by real world partisan cues about the 2020 election. These were especially reflected in then-President Donald Trump\u8217 s repeated claims that mail-in balloting was subject to fraud (e.g. Mounk, 2020).}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 The Internet-based survey experiment used an opt-in national sample of 4987 US adults between October 2\u8211 6, 2020. Our Lucid Theorem sample is not probability-based but is weighted to represent the general public along racial, gender, and age demographics.3 Relative to other quota sample providers, Lucid Theorem\u8217 s panel is appropriate for use in social science experiments (see Coppock & McClellan, 2019). Our sample\u8217 s age, gender, and racial demographics correspond closely with U.S. Census targets, and random treatment assignment did not correlate with any of these demographic items (or self-reported party identity). An examination of responses to our manipulation check protocol shows just under five percent of respondents did not correctly recall the topic of the social media material in their assigned condition. Removing these respondents from our analysis does not change the direction or robustness of our stated findings.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 As a measure of subjects\u8217  awareness of election fraud claims, we asked two pre-treatment questions: 1) how much they heard about how increased mail-in voting could affect the 2020 election, and 2) how much they heard about Trump\u8217 s criticism about the US Postal Service (USPS). 96% of subjects claimed to have heard \u8220 a lot\u8221  or \u8220 a little\u8221  about mail-voting issues. 90% claimed to have heard \u8220 a lot\u8221  or \u8220 a little\u8221  about the USPS criticism.4 These percentages underscore the extent to which subjects were pretreated with generally partisan cues concerning election integrity and legitimacy through the real-world events surrounding the 2020 election.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Subjects were randomly assigned to one of four groups, consisting of a control group and three treatment groups. Subjects in each assigned group were asked to click through a series of social media images and/or news stories (see Appendix for scenario material). Those in the control group viewed entertainment-themed social media content and served as our baseline. Treated subjects were exposed to a series of \u8220 voter fraud\u8221  news stories and social media messages raising allegations that mail-in-ballots were submitted on behalf of dead people. Labelled \u8220 the voting-dead scenario\u8221  (VDS), ballots being submitted on behalf of the deceased is among the most persistent myths about US election fraud and received national attention during and after the 2020 election (Holman & Lay, 2019).5}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 In constructing the Voting Dead Scenario, we believed that it was important to incorporate a wide range of sources and perspectives on the issue of election security. The information environment in United States has become highly splintered, with individuals getting their news from a wide range of media outlets including social media (Benkler et al., 2018). This was significant for our study because election officials not only had to counter qualms about election security, but also do so in a highly fractured and contentious information environment (e.g. Guess et al., 2018). This constitutes a tough test for crisis communicators that we did not think would be adequately captured by having subjects read a single story attributed to a source like }
{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 USA Today}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2
\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 .6 As such, we included both news media and tweets in our scenario materials. Furthermore, we purposefully included materials from sources spanning the media gamut and expressing varying degrees of skepticism towards the Voting Dead story from endorsing the conspiracy to fact-checkers to de-bunking the myth (see Appendix for experiment material).7 Consequently, while the volume of media sources on election security within the experiment outnumbered counter-communications from election officials, we also felt this to be important for an authentic test of electoral crisis communications (Images 1 and 2).Image 1.Example of VDS News Story.Image 2.Example of VDS Tweet.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Subjects in Treatment Group 1 were exposed to this VDS material and no other information (see Figure 1 for Design Structure). Those assigned to Treatment Groups 2 and 3 saw the VDS materials as well as crisis communication Tweets attributed to the Ohio Secretary of State\u8217 s (SoS) Office. The counter-messaging treatments were based on real world examples gathered from multiple Secretaries of Stated during the 2020 election (see Appendix for samples). Incidentally, this highlights a structural limitation in American electoral administration. Whereas electoral disinformation propagated by political and media elites can circulate at a national level (Clayton et al., 2021), primary responsibility for refuting such claims falls to state and county election officials whose messaging reach may not extend beyond their immediate geographic fiefdoms at best. The 2020 election embodied this disparity. While some Secretaries of State did receive national attention, they remained state officials whose counter-messaging may not resonate with national audiences (e.g. Karson & Robinson, 2020).Figure 1.Order of assigned treatment material.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 To capture this dynamic within our experiment, we selected the Ohio Secretary of State as our counter-messaging agent. This selection was made because of Ohio\u8217 s historic role as a swing state as well as the prominent role that the Ohio SoS played in real world crisis messaging leading up to the 2020 election (e.g. Searcey, 2020). For both Treatment Groups 2 and 3, the first SoS tweet preceded exposure to the VDS material. This initial pre-message consisted of a general warning to subjects to be wary of election disinformation. Following the VDS material, a counter-message from the SoS account appeared that directly refuted the VDS. The decision to include both pre- and post- messaging from the Secretary of State was informed by Clayton et al. (2020) and attempted to emulate best practices of pairing both general warnings on the dangers of disinformation (the pre-message) with efforts to debunk specific falsehoods (the post-message). Note that the pre and post SoS messages reflected real world communication by state SoS offices and similar entities prior to the 2020 election (see Appendix).}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 What differed between Treatment Groups 2 and 3 is the level of transparency in the post-VDS crisis communication from officials. Although the specific meaning A commitment to transparency is a common crisis response theme, and communication scholars link public perceptions of transparency to perceived information quality (Coombs & Holladay, 2002; Holland et al., 2018; Schnackenberg & Tomlinson, 2016). As such, we adopted two different post-scenario messages reflecting the different degrees of message transparency. What we term the SoS\u8217 s \u8220 low transparency\u8221  message (Treatment Group 2) simply stresses Ohio\u8217 s successful record of conducting secure elections. This is considered \u8220 low-transparency\u8221  because it does not provide any information or insights into election security and to be seen as transparent actors must be willing to provide information to stakeholders (Holland et al., 2018; Bushman et al., 2004; Kundeliene & Leitoniene, 2015).}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 By comparison the \u8220 high transparency\u8221  message for Treatment Group 3 outlines specific and proactive measures that the SoS took to secure the 2020 vote. By providing this detailed information the \u8220 high transparency\u8221  treatment evidenced high information quality based on clarity and disclosure (Craft & Heim 2018) which are both core attributes of message transparency (Schnackenberg & Tomlinson, 2016). The differing levels of crisis messaging transparency enables an assessment of whether any intervention by election officials is effective at countering disinformation as well as potential insights into whether elite transparency in crisis messaging boosts public confidence in election legitimacy (Images 3 and 4).Image 3.Low Transparency Message (TG2).Image 4.High Transparency Message (TG3).}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Balance checks in Table 1 show that treatment assignment is uncorrelated with subject race, gender, age, education level, partisanship. An attention check showed treated subjects recalled the VDS material (and SoS messages, in Treatment Groups 2 and 3). Overall, 96% of treated subjects correctly identified the crux of the VDS (and SoS messages, applicable for TG2 and TG3). Following exposure to their assigned material, all subjects were asked a series of survey questions about their views on election procedures. These questions were drawn from public surveys fielded throughout 2020 about the US election (see Appendix for survey questions and sources).Table 1.Random Assignment Balance Table: Mean(SD)/}
