\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Replying with 1\u8211 4 Likert response scales, subjects were asked about 1) level of concern that changes in voting rules due to COVID make it easy to cast fraudulent ballots (4 = \u8220 very concerned\u8221 ), 2) perceived frequency of voter fraud in the 2020 election (4 = \u8220 very often\u8221 ), 3) concern about tampering with mail during the 2020 election (4 = \u8220 very concerned\u8221 ), 4) perceived amount of fraud if people are allowed to vote by mail in 2020 (4 = \u8220 a great deal\u8221 ). We did not test for whether or not our respondents believed the disinformation presented in the VDS scenario. Instead, our dependent variable questions were designed to capture our respondents\u8217  beliefs about the soundness of election procedures and how this sentiment was affected by our treatment groups. The questions have high inter-item covariance, with a Cronbach\u8217 s alpha of .89. This makes the four items ideal for use in an additive score we label the Delegitimization Index. The higher the Index score (showing subjects have increased concern about election procedures), the less confidently they view the legitimacy of election practices (hence the Index name).}
