\ltrch\fs24\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\cell}{\trowd\irow9\irowband9\lastrow\ltrrow\trqc\trgaph108\trpaddl108\trpaddfl3\trbrdrt\trbrdrl\trbrdrb\trbrdrr\trbrdrh\trbrdrv\trftsWidth1\trautofit0\trwWidthB0\trftsWidthB3\trpaddt0\trpaddft3\trpaddb0\trpaddfb3\trpaddr108\trpaddfr3\trrh0\trleft-108\clwWidth3360\clftsWidth3
\clvertalt\clpadl20\clpadfl3\clpadb20\clpadfb3\clpadt108\clpadft3\clpadr108\clpadfr3\clbrdrl\brdrnone\clbrdrr\brdrnone\clbrdrt\brdrnone\clbrdrb\brdrnone\cldgll\brdrnone\cldglu\brdrnone\cellx3252\clwWidth1920\clftsWidth3\clvertalt\clpadl20\clpadfl3\clpadb20
\clpadfb3\clpadt108\clpadft3\clpadr108\clpadfr3\clbrdrl\brdrnone\clbrdrr\brdrnone\clbrdrt\brdrnone\clbrdrb\brdrnone\cldgll\brdrnone\cldglu\brdrnone\cellx5172\clwWidth1680\clftsWidth3\clvertalt\clpadl20\clpadfl3\clpadb20\clpadfb3\clpadt108\clpadft3\clpadr108
\clpadfr3\clbrdrl\brdrnone\clbrdrr\brdrnone\clbrdrt\brdrnone\clbrdrb\brdrnone\cldgll\brdrnone\cldglu\brdrnone\cellx6852\clwWidth2160\clftsWidth3\clvertalt\clpadl20\clpadfl3\clpadb20\clpadfb3\clpadt108\clpadft3\clpadr108\clpadfr3\clbrdrl\brdrnone\clbrdrr\brdrnone
\clbrdrt\brdrnone\clbrdrb\brdrnone\cldgll\brdrnone\cldglu\brdrnone\cellx9012\tbllkhdrrows\tbllkhdrcols\tbllknocolband\rtlch\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\fs24\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\row}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0
\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Importantly, the treatment effects of altered perceptions towards the election (in this case increased concerns about election procedures) are only distinguishable from the control. In terms of the delegitimacy index, there were no statistically significant differences between TG1 (VDS only), TG2 (low-transparency), or TG3 (high-transparency). This suggests that while exposure to electoral disinformation has a harmful effect on perceptions of election security, exposure to counter-messaging (regardless of transparency level) does not have a restorative or corrective effect. The lack of an ameliorating effects from the SoS interventions (H2) or any distinction in the messages\u8217  effectiveness based on transparency (H3) augurs poorly for the potential of official to counter disinformation\u8217 s effects on electoral perceptions.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 The overall lack of corrective effects from SoS messaging is clear, but this result may be tempered by partisan considerations. H4 expects there to be partisan differences in the Index scores, so our next analysis compares treatment effects by splitting subjects into partisan groups (i.e., Republicans and Democrats). For this analysis, we asked those identifying as \u8220 independents\u8221  which of the two major parties they lean closest to. This approach reflects the reality that few \u8220 independents\u8221  are actually unaligned with the political party (Abramowitz & Webster, 2018; Skelley, 2021). Using this approach, we classified 1888 subjects as Republican and 1717 as Democrat (see Table 1 for subject distribution across assigned treatment groups). Classifying \u8220 leaners\u8221  in this manner also makes for a clearer interpretation of treatment effects by party ID.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 If information selectivity based on partisanship and political cues has the effect that Druckman et al. (2013) found, the SoS messaging should be overwhelmed by partisan influence- irrespective of message transparency levels. This is generally what we find. Figure 2 shows Delegitimization Index scores by party affiliation. We see a clear difference in the mean scores between the two partisan groups, with self-identified Republicans having statistically higher Index values across all three treatment groups (i.e., meaning they have higher perceptions of election delegitimacy), including the two groups with counter-messaging. And, as evidence that the real-world pre-treatment from Trump and related elite rhetoric impacted subjects, even Republicans in the control group show a half point higher index score than their Democratic counterparts (despite not being exposed to any VDS material).Figure 2.TE effects on Delegit index score.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Looking specifically at the Index scores in Figure 2 we see that, among Republicans, there is a slight Index score increase for the No-SoS condition, with the counter messages showing slightly lower scores (though these differences are statistically indistinguishable). This may be some consolation in that the SoS appearance in the transparency treatments did not erode election perceptions among GOP subjects, but the lack of statistical effect in reassuring voters is certainly out of step with the literature\u8217 s expectations.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Democrats had a statistically lower Index scores across all our groupings indicating greater confidence in elections. However, Democrats\u8217  electoral faith did appear to be shaken by the disinformation campaign. While not statistically significant, the introduction of VDS images eroded Democratic sentiment towards election legitimacy. Moreover, these declines were not fully remedied by the SoS counter-messaging. These treatment effects largely paralleled Republicans and suggest that while partisans had different starting points in their thinking about electoral procedures in 2020, the deleterious effects of disinformation within our study and the inadequacies of the SoS responses were broadly similar.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb240\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\ql\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Conclusions: I\u8217 ll Send an SOS to the World}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{
\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 The objective of this study was to see if best practices from crisis communications could be leveraged by election officials to buoy popular faith in election procedures in the face of a dedicated campaign of electoral disinformation. Based on our experiment, the answer appears to be an unfortunate \u8220 no.\u8221  In all our treatment groups, the presence of disinformation in the form of the VDS had a corrosive effect on perceptions of election security. Subsequent counter-messaging from election officials failed to correct this decline. There were no statistically significant improvements in confidence in electoral practices between our treatments with SoS responses and subjects who received no corrective messaging at all. These results complement the findings of Berlinski et al. (2021) and emphasizes both the harmful effects of disinformation attacks on election procedures as well as the inability of counter-messaging to stem this damage in politicized atmospheres. Notably, throughout our experiment, including within the control group, Republicans were statistically more skeptical of election security. This reflected the partisan posture towards election security in 2020, but it is worth remembering that Democrats were more suspect of the 2016 vote (Clement, 2016) and that festering concerns about electoral legitimacy have bipartisan ramifications (Beaulieu, 2014).}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Furthermore, our attempt to leverage transparency as an electoral crisis messaging strategy also appears to have foundered. Not only did both SoS treatments fail to improve popular perceptions, but there was also no significant distinction between the high- and low- transparency messages. While the provision of high-quality information in a clear and open manner has been effective in other arenas, our study indicates that such advantages may be limited by a politicized environment with competing cues. Crisis communications may yet hold important lessons for officials, but our study nevertheless highlights the manifold challenge that strategies like transparency face in a fractured and politicized information ecosystem.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Although our findings highlight the obstacles that election officials face in assuaging anxieties about elections, it is also important to consider the limitations of our study and their implications. Significantly, our experiment was purposefully designed as a tough test for counter-messaging. Subjects were exposed to a large number of stories and tweets all concerning election security and possible vulnerabilities. While we included a range of perspectives in the VDS materials, even stories debunking the voting dead myth may have had a \u8220 don\u8217 t think about elephants\u8221  effect of heightening concerns about election security. Consequently, while our SoS interventions were not effective, these null findings may be the result of framing effects (Druckman, 2001). Likewise, while our particular SoS messages were not successful countermeasures, they are by no means definitive. Our SoS messages were presented independently from other materials, but there was no subsequent manipulation check to confirm that the messages were read and digested by respondents. Additionally, alternative strategies for discussing election security such as repeated interactions as well as mechanisms like video messages or press briefings, may prove more effective messaging tools and are worth exploring. Indeed, if the aftermath of the 2020 vote is any indication, election officials have only just begun to deal with the messaging challenges surrounding election legitimacy (Pennycook & Rand, 2021).}
